<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henri Bergson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1859">1859</a>–<a href="/years/1941">1941</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Henri-Louis Bergson</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henri Bergson was a French philosopher, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, who argued that immediate experience and intuition matter more than abstract rationalism and science for understanding reality. His four principal works were Time and Free Will (1889), Matter and Memory (1896), Creative Evolution (1907) and The Two Sources of Morality and Religion (1932). He held the chair of Greek and Roman philosophy and then that of modern philosophy at the Collège de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, where the public attended his open courses in large numbers. He received the 1927 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature, and his popularity caused controversy in France, where his views were seen as opposed to the Republic's secular and scientific attitude.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henri-bergson.jpg" alt="Henri Bergson" width="330" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henri Manuel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henri_Bergson_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sigmund Freud placed the unconscious at the centre of mental life, and <b>Henri Bergson</b>’s idea of subjective time deeply influenced later novelists.</blockquote><a href="/movement/modernism">Modernism · Background</a></li></ul></section>
